Fix l and r. If h[l] <= h[r], the best container still using l as its left wall has height at most h[l] and width at most (r - l), so it cannot beat the current area. Every r' < r yields: area = min(h[l], h[r']) * (r' - l) <= h[l] * (r - l) Hence l can be discarded forever — monotonicity turns the O(n²) search into a single O(n) sweep measuring only n-1 rectangles. =====================================================================

Fix l and r. If h[l] <= h[r], the best possible container that still uses l as its LEFT wall has height at most h[l] and width at most (r - l) — so it cannot beat the current area. Every r' < r yields area = min(h[l], h[r']) * (r' - l) <= h[l] * (r - l). Hence l can be discarded forever: this is the monotonicity argument that turns the O(n²) search into a single O(n) sweep. Each step we shrink the "candidate rectangle" of wall pairs by one column, and the answer is the maximum over the n-1 rectangles we measure. ===================================================================== /

