With sorted input, any triplet can be emitted in sorted order (a ≤ b ≤ c). Two different (i,j,k) index triples produce the same sorted VALUE triple only when the same values repeat — and the skip-duplicates rule jumps PAST those repeats, so the exact same value triple can never be recorded twice. Python's set-of-tuples achieves the same by hashing the sorted tuple; the hash table underneath (dict/set = open addressing, linear probing) is exactly: h(k, i) = (hash(k) + i) mod m ,  i = 0, 1, 2, ... while C++ unordered_set chains colliding keys per bucket. =====================================================================

