p = "c*" against s = "cccc": the '*' must decide: zero copies → drop "c*" (dp[i][j-2]); one more copy → consume one matching char and KEEP "c*" (dp[i-1][j]). The OR lets ONE '*' absorb any run length, including zero. Table is (|s|+1)×(|p|+1) so the empty-string prefixes are representable. =====================================================================

p = "c*" against s = "ccca": at dp[4][2] (s="cccc"? no — s[0..3] = "cccc", p = "c*") the '*' must decide: • zero copies → dp[4][0] (drop "c*" entirely, keep all of s); • one more copy → s[3]=='c' == p[0] → dp[3][2] (consume one 'c', pattern stays "c*" so it may repeat). The OR is what lets one '*' absorb ANY run length — including zero. Table shape: (|s|+1) × (|p|+1) so index i/j means "first i / first j characters consumed" and the empty-string prefixes are representable. ===================================================================== /

